# ── Rocky docker-compose environment ─────────────────────────────
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Every variable the compose file
# interpolates (${VAR}) is documented here. Defaults are applied in
# compose for the optional overrides, so only DB_HOST/POSTGRES_PASSWORD/
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET/ROCKY_DOMAIN/TUNNEL_TOKEN are mandatory for a VM deploy.
#
# Two connection models coexist:
#   1) docker-compose: builds DATABASE_URL internally from DB_HOST/DB_PORT/
#      POSTGRES_PASSWORD — you DO NOT set DATABASE_URL in .env for compose.
#   2) Local dev (pnpm dev, Drizzle Studio): uses DATABASE_URL directly
#      (Section 4). Set it when running outside compose.
#
# ──── 1. Public domain (Cloudflare) ──────────────────────────────
# Dedicated tertiary subdomain of your Cloudflare zone, e.g. rocky.tehno.party.
# Drives api./admin./docs.<domain> hostnames; compose defaults to
# rocky.tehno.party. The Better Auth cookie domain derives from this
# (AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN defaults to .<domain>), so it must be the shared
# parent of api./admin./docs - NOT the bare zone if you have other subdomains.
ROCKY_DOMAIN=rocky.tehno.party

# ──── 2. Database (PostgreSQL + PostGIS) ────────────────────────
# In production/staging the DB is external (separate host/VM).
# For single-machine local dev, start the bundled db service:
#   docker compose --profile local-db up db
#
# DB_HOST is the network location reachable from the api container.
#   - external VM: set to the VM LAN IP, e.g. 192.168.1.109
#   - local docker: leave as "db" (the compose service name)
#   - local bare-metal: set to localhost (and start postgres natively)
DB_HOST=192.168.1.109
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=tbot
# Database password: used by the api service as PG_PASS and by the local
# db service as POSTGRES_PASSWORD. Must match the target DB.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=GAtBXJ9ykOZdRqiGBAZtNeLP4nTdmqFtSdMMdEAho9

# ──── 3. Better Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared by api + web (server-side session validation). Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=change_me_generate_with_openssl_rand_base64_32

# ──── 4. Local dev tooling (outside docker-compose) ──────────────
# These are NOT consumed by docker-compose (it derives them from the
# vars above). Set them when running pnpm dev, Drizzle Kit, etc.
# `packages/database` reads DATABASE_URL; the NestJS dev server reads
# PG_HOST/PG_PORT/PG_USER/PG_PASS/PG_DB from apps/api/src/config.ts.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://tbot:change_me_strong_password@192.168.1.109:5432/tbot
PG_HOST=192.168.1.109
PG_PORT=5432
PG_USER=tbot
PG_PASS=change_me_strong_password
PG_DB=tbot

# Runtime flags required by apps/api/src/config.ts. The zod enum has
# NO default, so ENVIRONMENT MUST be set for pnpm dev to start:
#   ENVIRONMENT = dev|production|test|staging
# VERSION is required (zod default "dev" if unset).
# NODE_ENV=development enables Drizzle query logging.
ENVIRONMENT=dev
VERSION=dev
NODE_ENV=development

# ──── 5. Cloudflare Tunnel Token ────────────────────────────────
# Token for the cloudflared compose service. Obtain it via the
# bundled bootstrap script (uses Cloudflare API, no interactive CLI):
#
#   CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=<api-token> CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=<acct-id> \
#     ROCKY_DOMAIN=rocky.tehno.party \
#     bash scripts/setup-tunnel.sh
#
# The script creates the tunnel, pushes ingress rules, ensures DNS,
# and writes the token into .env. See scripts/setup-tunnel.sh.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=change_me_cloudflare_tunnel_token

# ──── 6. Drizzle Studio (optional docker compose --profile dev) ──
DRIZZLE_MASTER_PASS=change_me

# ──── 7. Cloudflare Access (the "shield") ────────────────────────
# Web admin: the browser logs into Cloudflare Access (TOTP/2FA, no Google)
# and passes via the CF_Authorization cookie — no code change needed.
# Mobile app: uses a Cloudflare Access SERVICE TOKEN (machine auth). Set
# these in apps/mob/.env (inlined as EXPO_PUBLIC_* at build time), NOT here:
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=<cf-service-token-client-id>
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=<cf-service-token-secret>
# Create the token in Cloudflare Zero Trust > Access > Service Tokens.
# Then make the api hostname's Access group (rocky-api) allow that token
# (+ authenticated users, so the web browser also reaches api).

# ──── 8. Optional VM overrides (defaults shown) ──────────────────
# These derive from ROCKY_DOMAIN by default; override only if needed.
# WEB_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.<domain>
# AUTH_BASE_URL=https://admin.<domain>
# CORS_ORIGINS=https://admin.<domain>
# TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://admin.<domain>,mobile://
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://api.<domain>
# AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.<domain>
# VERSION=production
# NODE_ENV=production
# ENVIRONMENT=production
# API_URL=http://api:8080    # web SSR proxy target (compose sets it)

# ──── 9. Notes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# - Email suite is OFF (not configured).
# - Redis is not used by Rocky (Postgres handles everything).
# - Published ports are removed; the Cloudflare Tunnel is the only ingress.
# - Configure public hostnames in Cloudflare Dashboard > Networks > Tunnels.
